    content: `"Venomous" and "poisonous" are often used interchangeably in casual speech, but biologists draw a specific, consistent distinction between them based on how the toxin is delivered. An organism is venomous if it actively injects its toxin into another organism, typically through a bite, sting, or spine — snakes, scorpions, bees, and jellyfish are venomous, because they deliver toxin directly, usually to attack prey or defend themselves. An organism is poisonous if it causes harm when touched or, more commonly, eaten, passively releasing toxin without any dedicated delivery mechanism — poison dart frogs, many species of pufferfish, and numerous toxic plants and mushrooms are poisonous rather than venomous, since the toxin only takes effect if something else touches or ingests them. A small number of animals are technically both: the poison dart frog is poisonous through its skin secretions, and some snakes are both venomous (through their bite) and mildly poisonous if eaten, since their venom-producing glands remain toxic in the meat. Using the terms precisely can matter in a wilderness safety context, since a purely poisonous animal generally isn't dangerous to simply observe or hold gently, while a venomous one can be dangerous through direct contact with its delivery mechanism.`,
