    content: `Hibernation is a state of dramatically reduced metabolic activity, body temperature, heart rate, and breathing rate that some animals enter to survive long periods of cold and food scarcity, allowing them to burn far less energy than they would staying active. True deep hibernators, like ground squirrels and many bats, can drop their body temperature close to freezing and their heart rate to just a few beats per minute for weeks at a time, waking periodically to briefly warm up before returning to torpor. Interestingly, bears are sometimes excluded from the strictest definition of "true hibernators" by some biologists, since their body temperature drops only moderately compared to smaller hibernators and they can wake and move relatively quickly if disturbed — though most scientists today still classify bear winter dormancy as a legitimate, if distinct, form of hibernation given how dramatically their metabolism, heart rate, and breathing slow down for months without eating, drinking, or eliminating waste. Torpor is a shorter-term, less extreme version of the same strategy, used by some birds and small mammals overnight or for a few days at a time rather than an entire season. Brumation is the reptile and amphibian equivalent of hibernation — since reptiles are already cold-blooded and don't actively regulate their body temperature the way mammals do, brumating reptiles like turtles and snakes simply become extremely sluggish and largely inactive as ambient temperatures drop, sometimes rousing briefly on unusually warm winter days to drink water before returning to dormancy.`,
