    content: `Camouflage helps an animal avoid detection by blending into its surroundings, using coloration, patterning, or body shape that matches its habitat — a stick insect resembling a twig, a snowshoe hare turning white in winter, or a flounder changing its skin pattern to match the seafloor it's resting on. Mimicry is a related but distinct strategy in which one species evolves to resemble another species (rather than its background environment) for a survival advantage. In Batesian mimicry, a harmless species evolves to resemble a dangerous or unpalatable one — the harmless hoverfly's yellow-and-black stripes mimic a stinging wasp's warning coloration, deterring predators without the hoverfly needing any actual sting. In Müllerian mimicry, two or more genuinely dangerous or unpalatable species evolve to resemble each other, reinforcing a shared warning signal so predators only need to learn to avoid one pattern to avoid them all — several unrelated species of toxic butterflies displaying similar wing patterns is a classic example. Chameleons, contrary to popular belief, don't primarily change color for camouflage — their most dramatic color shifts are mainly used for communication and signaling mood, temperature regulation, or readiness to mate, achieved by adjusting microscopic crystal structures in their skin that reflect different wavelengths of light, a mechanism unrelated to the pigment-based color changes used by animals like octopuses.`,
