    content: `A keystone species is one whose presence or absence has an outsized effect on its ecosystem relative to its actual population size or biomass — remove it, and the whole ecological community can shift dramatically, similar to how removing the keystone of a stone arch causes the entire structure to collapse. One of the most cited real-world examples is the reintroduction of gray wolves to Yellowstone National Park in 1995, after they had been eliminated from the park decades earlier; wolves reduced and redistributed the local elk population, which had been overgrazing young willow and aspen trees along riverbanks, and the recovery of that vegetation is credited with helping stabilize riverbanks, support beaver populations (which build dams that create wetland habitat for many other species), and generally increase biodiversity — an example of what ecologists call a "trophic cascade," where effects ripple outward through multiple levels of a food web. Sea otters play a similarly outsized role in Pacific kelp forest ecosystems by preying on sea urchins, which would otherwise overgraze and destroy kelp forests if left unchecked, since kelp forests themselves support enormous numbers of fish and other marine species. Other commonly cited keystone species include elephants (which knock down trees and create clearings that shape entire savanna landscapes) and beavers (whose dam-building creates wetland habitats used by countless other species).`,
