    content: `Echolocation is a biological form of sonar that certain animals, most notably bats and toothed whales (including dolphins), use to navigate and hunt by emitting sound pulses and interpreting the echoes that bounce back off nearby objects. Most bats emit rapid, high-frequency ultrasonic calls — generally well above the range of human hearing — through their mouth or nose, and by analyzing tiny differences in the timing, pitch, and intensity of the returning echoes, a bat can build a remarkably detailed real-time picture of its surroundings, precise enough to detect and catch a small flying insect in complete darkness. As a bat closes in on prey, it typically increases its call rate dramatically (a pattern researchers call a "feeding buzz") to get more frequent updates on the target's exact position. Dolphins and other toothed whales use a comparable system, generating clicking sounds using structures near their nasal passages and a fatty organ in their forehead called the "melon," which helps focus the sound into a directional beam; returning echoes are received partly through the lower jaw, which conducts sound to the inner ear. Dolphin echolocation is sensitive enough to distinguish between objects of similar size and shape and can even detect some information about an object's internal structure, useful for both navigation and hunting fish in murky or dark water.`,
