    content: `Honeybees communicate the location of a good food source to their hivemates through a remarkable behavior known as the "waggle dance," first decoded scientifically by Austrian zoologist Karl von Frisch, work for which he shared a Nobel Prize in 1973. A returning forager bee performs a figure-eight movement on the vertical surface of the honeycomb inside the dark hive, with a central "waggle run" in which she vibrates her body rapidly while walking in a straight line, before circling back to repeat the pattern. The angle of the waggle run relative to straight up on the comb indicates the direction of the food source relative to the sun's current position outside, while the duration of the waggle run correlates with the distance to the source — a longer waggle indicates a farther food source. Other bees in the hive, sensing the dance through touch, vibration, and even scent cues, can then fly directly to a food source they've never personally visited, based purely on this encoded information, making it one of the most sophisticated forms of symbolic communication documented in any non-human animal.`,
