    content: `Most salmon species are anadromous, meaning they hatch in freshwater rivers and streams, migrate downstream to spend most of their adult lives feeding and growing in the ocean, and then migrate back upstream, often to the exact same stretch of river where they themselves hatched, to spawn. This upstream journey can involve swimming hundreds of miles against strong currents and leaping up waterfalls and rapids, an enormously energy-demanding feat that most salmon accomplish without feeding at all during the final stretch, relying entirely on fat reserves built up during their ocean years. In several Pacific salmon species (like sockeye and chinook), this reproductive effort is fatal: after spawning, the exhausted adults die within days or weeks, and their decomposing bodies actually deliver a significant pulse of ocean-derived nutrients into freshwater and surrounding forest ecosystems, supporting everything from aquatic insects to bears and eagles that feed on the spawning run, and even measurably enriching the growth of nearby streamside trees. Like sea turtles, salmon are believed to navigate back to their natal stream partly using a magnetic sense combined with a highly refined sense of smell, allowing them to detect the unique chemical "scent" of their home stream's water even after years spent in the open ocean.`,
