    title: 'Hedgehogs vs. Porcupines: Not the Same Animal',
    category: 'Biology',
    keywords: ['hedgehog vs porcupine difference', 'are hedgehogs and porcupines related', 'quill defense animals', 'do hedgehogs shoot their quills'],
    content: `Hedgehogs and porcupines are frequently confused because both are covered in defensive spines, but they belong to entirely different mammal groups and are not closely related. Hedgehogs are small insectivore-adjacent mammals (most closely related to shrews and moles) covered in thousands of relatively short, stiff spines, and their primary defense is curling into a tight ball, using powerful muscles to pull their spiny back over their vulnerable underside, presenting predators with an unappealing spiky sphere rather than attacking directly. Porcupines are rodents — more closely related to squirrels and guinea pigs than to hedgehogs — and are generally much larger, with longer, more prominent quills that can, in many species, detach relatively easily and lodge painfully in a predator's skin upon contact, aided by microscopic backward-facing barbs on the quill tips that make them difficult to remove once embedded. Neither animal, despite a persistent myth, can "shoot" or launch their quills through the air at a distance — porcupine quills simply detach easily on contact and hedgehog spines don't detach at all, remaining firmly attached to the animal at all times.`,
