    category: 'Biology',
    keywords: ['immortal jellyfish', 'turritopsis dohrnii', 'can jellyfish live forever', 'biologically immortal animal'],
    content: `Turritopsis dohrnii, a tiny jellyfish species native to the Mediterranean Sea (and now found more widely, likely spread via ship ballast water), is popularly nicknamed the "immortal jellyfish" for a genuinely unusual biological ability: when injured, stressed, aging, or starving, it can revert from its mature adult (medusa) form back into an earlier juvenile polyp stage — biologically resetting its life cycle rather than dying — a transformation process called transdifferentiation, in which existing specialized cells convert into different cell types entirely, effectively repeating development from a much earlier life stage. In principle, this means a single individual could theoretically repeat this reversal cycle indefinitely, which is the basis for the "immortal" nickname, though this doesn't mean the species is invincible or unkillable in any absolute sense — an individual can still be killed by a predator, disease, or unfavorable conditions before it gets the chance to revert. The species has become a genuine subject of interest for aging research, since understanding the cellular mechanism that allows it to functionally reverse its biological age could offer insight relevant to regenerative medicine, though translating this into any practical application for other species, including humans, remains a distant and highly uncertain prospect.`,
