    content: `Dolphins are widely regarded as among the most cognitively and socially sophisticated non-human animals, with large, complex brains relative to body size and behaviors that suggest advanced problem-solving, self-awareness, and social cooperation. Bottlenose dolphins are one of the few non-human species to pass mirror self-recognition tests, a marker researchers generally associate with a basic form of self-awareness, also passed by great apes, elephants, and magpies. Each bottlenose dolphin develops its own unique "signature whistle" early in life, which functions much like a name — other dolphins learn to recognize and even mimic a specific individual's signature whistle to get that individual's attention or refer to them, a form of vocal labeling documented in very few other species. Dolphins also display sophisticated cooperative hunting strategies, in some regions working together to herd fish into tight, easily caught groups, and some populations have been observed using tools, such as carrying a marine sponge on their beak (rostrum) to protect it while foraging for prey along a rough, potentially injury-prone seafloor — a learned behavior passed down primarily from mother to calf within specific dolphin communities rather than something all dolphins do instinctively.`,
